## Why This Is Asked

Tests a fresh DP angle: combine subproblem best within intervals with partition into exactly k blocks, ensuring non-overlap and order.

## Key Concepts

- Precompute bestSubarray(l, r) for all intervals.
- Knapsack-style DP over blocks: dp[i][t] = max over l of dp[l-1][t-1] + bestSubarray(l, i).
- Reconstruction: store the chosen l for each dp[i][t].
- Complexity: O(n^2 k) time, O(n^2) for bestSubarray and O(nk) for DP.

## Code Example

```javascript
function solve(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  // 1) bestSubarray(l, r)
  const best = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(n).fill(-Infinity));
  for (let l = 0; l < n; l++) {
    let maxEnding = 0;
    let bestSoFar = -Infinity;
    for (let r = l; r < n; r++) {
      maxEnding = Math.max(A[r], maxEnding + A[r]);
      bestSoFar = Math.max(bestSoFar, maxEnding);
      best[l][r] = bestSoFar;
    }
  }
  // 2) DP over i, t
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) dp[i][1] = best[0][i], par[i][1] = -2;
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
      let bestVal = -Infinity, bestL = -1;
      for (let l = t - 1; l <= i; l++) {
        const cand = (l - 1 >= 0 ? dp[l - 1][t - 1] : 0) + best[l][i];
        if (cand > bestVal) { bestVal = cand; bestL = l; }
      }
      dp[i][t] = bestVal;
      par[i][t] = bestL;
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return { bestValue: dp[n - 1][k], blocks: [] };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize memory to O(nk)?
- How would you adapt if blocks must be exactly length L or gaps have a minimum size?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP on subsequences with transition costs; requires deriving a 2D DP with O(n^2 m) time and backpointers. Edge cases: m=1 yields 0. Consider tie-breaking on reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP on subsequences; 2D states; O(n^2 m) time; backtracking via parents; rolling arrays for space.

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostSubseq(A, m){
  const n = A.length;
  const INF = 1e18;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, ()=>Array(m+1).fill(INF));
  const par = Array.from({length: n}, ()=>Array(m+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=0; i<n; i++) dp[i][1] = 0;
  for(let i=0; i<n; i++){
    for(let t=2; t<=m; t++){
      for(let p=0; p<i; p++){
        const val = dp[p][t-1] + Math.abs(A[i]-A[p]);
        if(val < dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = p; }
      }
    }
  }
  let best = INF, end = -1;
  for(let i=0; i<n; i++){ if(dp[i][m] < best){ best = dp[i][m]; end = i; } }
  const idx = []; let t=m, cur = end;
  while(t >= 1){ idx.push(cur); cur = par[cur][t]; t--; }
  idx.reverse();
  return {cost: best, indices: idx};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP over partitioning with a quadratic segment cost, requiring prefix sums, base cases, and reconstruction, plus discussion of optimizations.

## Key Concepts
- DP over partitions: dp[i][t] for first i elements into t segments
- Prefix sums for O(1) segment sums
- Quadratic segment cost and optimization (CHT or Divide-and-Conquer)
- Reconstructing segment boundaries with a prev table
- Time/space trade-offs and edge cases

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostPartition(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const S = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) S[i] = S[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const INF = 1e18;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n+1 }, () => Array(k+1).fill(INF));
  const prev = Array.from({ length: n+1 }, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k && t <= i; t++) {
      for (let j = 0; j < i; j++) {
        const diff = S[i] - S[j];
        const cost = diff * diff;
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + cost;
        if (val < dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          prev[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return { cost: dp[n][k] };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a less-traveled DP angle: maximizing a sum of subarray-specific statistics (distinct counts) with exact segmentation. Requires careful state design and backtracking for reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefix end i and segment count t
- Subarray distinct counts D(p,i-1)
- Backpointers for reconstruction
- Time: O(n^2 k), Space: O(nk)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxDistinctPartition(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  // Precompute distinct counts D[l][r]
  const D = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let l = 0; l < n; l++) {
    const seen = new Set();
    for (let r = l; r < n; r++) {
      seen.add(A[r]);
      D[l][r] = seen.size;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const bt = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) dp[i][1] = D[0][i - 1];
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
      for (let p = t - 1; p <= i - 1; p++) {
        const cand = dp[p][t - 1] + D[p][i - 1];
        if (cand > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = cand; bt[i][t] = p; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruct
  let i = n, t = k;
  const segs = [];
  while (t > 0) {
    const p = bt[i][t];
    segs.push([p, i - 1]);
    i = p; t--;
  }
  segs.reverse();
  return { best: dp[n][k], segments: segs };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize to O(nk) memory?
- How would you handle at most k segments instead of exactly k?
- Can you adapt to streaming data or reconstruct segments efficiently for large n?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to structure DP with prefix states, reconstruct decisions, and enforce a gap between segments — a common real-world pattern in budgeting or scheduling.

## Key Concepts
- Kadane variant to track best subarray ending at i
- 2D DP dp[i][t] for first i elements and t blocks
- Reconstructing endpoints with backpointers; gap handling

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const endSum = Array(n).fill(0);
  const endStart = Array(n).fill(0);
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if (i === 0 || endSum[i-1] + A[i] < A[i]) {
      endSum[i] = A[i];
      endStart[i] = i;
    } else {
      endSum[i] = endSum[i-1] + A[i];
      endStart[i] = endStart[i-1];
    }
  }
  // dp and backtracking would follow (omitted for brevity)
}
```

