## Why This Is Asked

This question tests a concrete DP pattern for selecting exactly k non-overlapping subarrays with a fixed per-subarray cost. It requires careful state definitions, reconstruction, and edge-case handling of negative sums.

## Key Concepts

- DP for k disjoint subarrays with end-state tracking
- Prefix sums or running sums to compute subarray value fast
- Reconstruction pointers to recover endpoints
- Time: O(nk); Space: O(nk) (with optimizations)

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length, NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const end = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      end[i][t] = NEG;
      if (t > 0) end[i][t] = Math.max(end[i-1][t] + A[i-1], dp[i-1][t-1] + A[i-1] - c);
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i-1][t], end[i][t]);
    }
  }
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How to optimize to O(nk) time and O(k) space?
- How handle cases where all values are negative or c is large?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests DP partitioning with a non-trivial per-segment cost that depends on both sum and min, plus reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Partition DP with exact k segments
- Precompute minVal(l,r) in O(n^2)
- Transition uses prefix sums and minVal; reconstruct with parent pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxCost(A, k){
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) pref[i] = pref[i-1] + A[i-1];
  // minVal[l][r]
  const minVal = Array.from({length:n+2}, ()=>Array(n+2).fill(0));
  for(let l=1;l<=n;l++){
    let m = A[l-1];
    minVal[l][l] = m;
    for(let r=l;r<=n;r++){
      m = (r===l) ? A[l-1] : Math.min(m, A[r-1]);
      minVal[l][r] = m;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){ dp[i][1] = (pref[i]-pref[0]) * minVal[1][i]; par[i][1] = 0; }
  for(let t=2;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=t;i<=n;i++){
      for(let p=t-1;p<=i-1;p++){
        const val = dp[p][t-1] + (pref[i]-pref[p]) * minVal[p+1][i];
        if(val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = p; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return {best: dp[n][k]};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests clean 0/1 DP layering with an additional teleport operation, requiring careful handling to avoid double-counting and efficient O(mn) time complexity with minimal extra memory overhead.

## Key Concepts
- 0/1 DP layering with an additional operation constraint
- Row/column prefix maxima for efficient teleport transition computation
- Path reconstruction under dual-state DP representation

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSum(grid) {
  const m = grid.length, n = grid[0].length;
  const dp0 = Array(m).fill().map(() => Array(n).fill(0));
  const dp1 = Array(m).fill().map(() => Array(n).fill(0));

  // Initialize first cell
  dp0[0][0] = grid[0][0];
  dp1[0][0] = grid[0][0];

  // Fill DP tables
  for (let i = 0; i < m; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < n; j++) {
      if (i === 0 && j === 0) continue;

      // Regular moves without teleport
      dp0[i][j] = Math.max(
        i > 0 ? dp0[i-1][j] : 0,
        j > 0 ? dp0[i][j-1] : 0
      ) + grid[i][j];

      // After teleport or using teleport now
      dp1[i][j] = Math.max(
        i > 0 ? dp1[i-1][j] : 0,
        j > 0 ? dp1[i][j-1] : 0,
        // Teleport from any cell in same row/column
        ...getTeleportOptions(dp0, i, j)
      ) + grid[i][j];
    }
  }

  return Math.max(dp0[m-1][n-1], dp1[m-1][n-1]);
}
```

